window.PHASE_DATA_LN=window.PHASE_DATA_LN||{};window.PHASE_DATA_LN[8]={"pages":[{"pdf":192,"printed":177,"ch":8,"text":"CHAPTER 8\n\nLayer 7: Research Paradigms and the\nPhilosophy of Economics\nBefore you start\nWhy this layer exists. Layers 1 to 6 gave you the content of the thesis. Layer 7 gives you the philosophical footing for the methodology chapter: what kind of knowledge claims you are entitled to make, and why. It is short, but examiners ask about it. Six terms to know first\n• Ontology: what exists; what the nature of (economic) reality is.\n• Epistemology: how we can know it; the relationship between researcher and subject.\n• Methodology vs methods: methodology is the logic of how you find out; methods are the tools (regression, interviews, stress tests).\n• Positivism / postpositivism: reality is out there and can be measured (positivism) or only imperfectly and probabilistically (postpositivism).\n• Critical realism: two meanings here. Lawson (via Bhaskar): a layered, open-system reality. Guba and Lincoln: a label for postpositivism.\n• Constructivism: realities are multiple and socially constructed; inquiry is interpretive. Map of the two readings Table 8.1. Before you start Reading\n\nRole\n\nSize and condition\n\nGuba and\nCompares four paradigms; gives you the vocabulary 13-page scan, read by OCR\nLincoln (1994) and the Table 6.1 to anchor your chapter\nLawson (1997) Argues economics neglects ontology; critiques\ndeductivism and econometric practice\n\n39-page excerpt only (preface,\nCh. 1, start of Ch. 2)\n\nSuggested reading order. Read Section 1 (Guba and Lincoln) first for the vocabulary, then Section 2 (Lawson) for the economics-specific argument, then Section 3 for quotables and how to write it into the thesis. About 40 minutes. One warning. The two authors use ”critical realism” differently, and Guba and Lincoln openly argue for constructivism. Neither is a neutral summary."},{"pdf":193,"printed":178,"ch":8,"text":"1. Guba and Lincoln (1994), Competing Paradigms in Qualitative\nResearch\nWhat it is. Chapter 6 of Denzin and Lincoln's Handbook of Qualitative Research (Sage, 1994), pp. 105 to 117. It compares four research paradigms (positivism, postpositivism, critical theory, constructivism) by asking each three questions: what is reality, how can we know it, and how do we find out. It is the most-cited paradigm chart in the social sciences. Read in full: the whole chapter, pp. 105 to 116 (p. 117 is the reference list). Your PDF is a photocopy scan without a text layer, so I read it by OCR and checked the key page (p. 109, with Table 6.1) against the page image. Page numbers are the Handbook's own printed pages. Bias to keep in mind: the authors state at once that they are committed to constructivism (p. 105), so the chapter argues from that side. Read the descriptions of positivism and postpositivism as how a constructivist sees them.\n\nThe argument in six moves\n1. Paradigm comes before method (p. 105). The authors argue ”questions of method are secondary to questions of paradigm, which we define as the basic belief system or worldview that guides the investigator, not only in choices of method but in ontologically and epistemologically fundamental ways.” Both qualitative and quantitative methods ”may be used appropriately with any research paradigm” (p. 105). So a regression is not automatically positivist, and an interview is not automatically constructivist.\n2. Why quantification dominated (pp. 105 to 106). Mathematics is the ”queen of sciences”; fields that quantify are called ”hard”; the social sciences copied them, partly after Mill's urging and partly for status (p. 106).\n3. Two kinds of critique of the ”received view” (pp. 106 to 107).\n• Intraparadigm (internal) critiques (p. 106): context stripping; exclusion of meaning and purpose; the gap between grand etic theory and local emic views; inapplicability of general data to individual cases (nomothetic vs idiographic); and exclusion of the discovery dimension. These can be met by adding qualitative data.\n• Extraparadigm (external) critiques (p. 107), which the authors say weigh more: the theory-ladenness of facts (facts are facts only within a theoretical framework), the underdetermination of theory (the problem of induction: ”a million white swans can never establish ... that all swans are white, one black swan can completely falsify it”), the value-ladenness of facts, and the interactive nature of the inquirer and the inquired-into (Heisenberg and Bohr cited as precedent).\n4. What a paradigm is (pp. 107 to 108). ”A paradigm may be viewed as a set of basic beliefs (or metaphysics) that deals with ultimates or first principles” (p. 107); the beliefs ”must be accepted simply on faith (however well argued)” (p. 107). Each paradigm answers three linked questions, and each answer constrains the next (p. 108):\n• Ontological: what is the form and nature of reality?\n• Epistemological: what is the relationship between knower and known?\n• Methodological: how can the inquirer find out? ”The methodological question\ncannot be reduced to a question of methods; methods must be fitted to a predetermined methodology” (p. 108)."},{"pdf":194,"printed":179,"ch":8,"text":"5. Paradigms are human constructions (p. 108). There is no ultimate proof of one over another; advocates ”must rely on persuasiveness and utility rather than proof” (p. 108). The authors add that this does not doom them to radical relativism.\n6. The four paradigms compared (Table 6.1, p. 109; elaborated pp. 109 to 112). Shown below in my wording of their table. Table 8.2. Guba and Lincoln (1994): The argument in six moves Positivism\n\nPostpositivism\n\nOntologyNaive realism: a real Critical realism: a real\nreality that can be\nreality, but only\napprehended\nimperfectly and\nprobabilistically\napprehended\n\nCritical theory et al.\n\nConstructivism\n\nHistorical realism: a\n”virtual” reality shaped\nby social, political,\ncultural, economic,\nethnic and gender\nvalues, crystallised over\ntime\n\nRelativism:\nlocal and\nspecific\nconstructed\nrealities\n\nEpistemology\nDualist and\nobjectivist; findings\ntrue\n\nModified\nTransactional and\ndualist/objectivist; critical subjectivist; findings\ntradition and community; value-mediated\nfindings probably true\n\nMethodology\nExperimental and\nmanipulative;\nverification of\nhypotheses; chiefly\nquantitative\n\nModified experimental;\ncritical multiplism;\nfalsification of hypotheses;\nmay include qualitative\nmethods\n\nDialogic and dialectical\n\nTransactional\nand subjectivist;\nfindings created\nHermeneutical\nand dialectical\n\n• Positivism (pp. 109 to 110): reality ”driven by immutable natural laws and mechanisms”, knowledge as ”time- and context-free generalizations”, research ”can, in principle, converge on the 'true' state of affairs”; the stance is ”reductionist and deterministic” (p. 109). Inquiry is ”as through a one-way mirror” (p. 110).\n• Postpositivism (p. 110): reality exists but is ”only imperfectly apprehendable because of basically flawed human intellectual mechanisms”; the label critical realism comes from Cook and Campbell (1979). Objectivity ”remains a 'regulatory ideal'”; replicated findings are ”probably true”; methodology uses ”critical multiplism (a refurbished version of triangulation)” to falsify rather than verify.\n• Critical theory (pp. 110 to 111): a blanket term including neo-Marxism, feminism, materialism and participatory inquiry. Structures were once plastic and then ”crystallized (reified)” into what is now taken as natural. The investigator's values inevitably shape findings, which ”effectively challenges the traditional distinction between ontology and epistemology” (p. 110).\n• Constructivism (pp. 110 to 111): realities are ”multiple, intangible mental constructions” held by individuals and groups; constructions are ”not more or less 'true,' in any absolute sense, but simply more or less informed and/or sophisticated”. The aim is a ”consensus construction” more informed than its predecessors (p. 111).\n• Where the real differences lie (p. 111): ”It is the ontological position that most differentiates constructivism from the other three paradigms”, and ”It is their epistemological positions that most differentiate critical theory and constructivism from the other two paradigms.”"},{"pdf":195,"printed":180,"ch":8,"text":"Ten practical issues (Table 6.2, p. 112; discussion pp. 112 to 116)\nThe authors show that paradigm choice changes how you work. Selected rows:\nTable 8.3. Guba and Lincoln (1994): Ten practical issues (Table 6.2, p. 112; discussion pp. 112 to 116) Issue\n\nPositivism and postpositivism Critical theory\n\nConstructivism\n\nAim of\ninquiry (p.\n113)\n\nExplanation, leading to\nprediction and control\n\nCritique and\ntransformation;\nrestitution and\nemancipation\n\nUnderstanding and\nreconstruction\n\nNature of\nknowledge\n(p. 113)\n\nVerified hypotheses as facts\nor laws (postpositivism:\nnon-falsified, probable)\n\nStructural and historical\ninsights\n\nConsensus constructions;\nmultiple knowledges can\ncoexist\n\nAccumulation Accretion, ”building blocks”\n(pp. 113 to in an ”edifice of knowledge”\n114)\n\nHistorical revision\n\nMore informed constructions;\ntransfer by vicarious\nexperience (case studies)\n\nQuality\ncriteria (p.\n114)\n\nInternal validity, external\nvalidity, reliability,\nobjectivity\n\nHistorical situatedness;\nerosion of ignorance;\nstimulus to action\n\nTrustworthiness (credibility,\ntransferability, dependability,\nconfirmability) and\nauthenticity criteria\n\nValues (p.\n114)\n\nExcluded (”value free”)\n\nIncluded, formative\n\nIncluded, formative\n\nVoice (p.\n115)\n\n”Disinterested scientist”\n\n”Transformative\nintellectual”\n\n”Passionate participant”\n\n• A point worth quoting (p. 114): the conventional rigor criteria ”depend on the realist ontological position; without the assumption, isomorphism of findings with reality can have no meaning, strict generalizability ... is impossible, stability cannot be assessed ... and objectivity cannot be achieved”. This is the authors' argument that validity language is tied to a paradigm.\n• Constructivist criteria (p. 114): the trustworthiness criteria parallel the positivist ones, which the authors admit makes them ”suspect”, and they say quality criteria for constructivism are ”not well resolved”.\n• Ethics (pp. 114 to 115): for the first two paradigms ethics is ”extrinsic” (policed by committees), and the realist ontology provides ”a tilt toward the use of deception” in some cases; for the other two it is ”intrinsic”.\n• Training (p. 115): novices must be ”resocialized” from the received view, which requires thorough schooling in positivism and postpositivism first.\n• Commensurability (pp. 115 to 116): positivists and postpositivists think paradigms can be accommodated. Critical theorists and constructivists say they are incommensurable: ”Realism and relativism, value freedom and value boundedness, cannot coexist in any internally consistent metaphysical system” (p. 116). Resolution awaits ”a metaparadigm that renders the older, accommodated paradigms not less true, but simply irrelevant” (p. 116).\n• Hegemony (p. 116): postpositivists ”control publication outlets, funding sources, promotion and tenure mechanisms, dissertation committees” (p. 116). Worth noting"},{"pdf":196,"printed":181,"ch":8,"text":"when you discuss how economics treats qualitative work.\n\nConclusion (p. 116)\nThe ”paradigm wars” metaphor is ”overdrawn”, and the authors favour dialogue. Closing sentence: ”Paradigm issues are crucial; no inquirer, we maintain, ought to go about the business of inquiry without being clear about just what paradigm informs and guides his or her approach.” Note 1 (p. 116) says postpositivism is ”damage control” rather than a reformulation, and credits Kuhn (1962) for making the need for a paradigm shift visible.\n\nHow to use it in the thesis\n• State your paradigm in one paragraph. Your thesis models capital adequacy against climate exposure with panel data and event-style tests, and builds a green/brown classification. In Guba and Lincoln's terms, that is postpositivist: a real world, imperfectly known, tested by falsification, with objectivity as a regulatory ideal (pp. 109 to 110). Say so, and say what that means for what you can claim (probable, not proven).\n• Watch the two meanings of 'critical realism'. Guba and Lincoln use the label for postpositivism (Cook and Campbell, p. 110). Lawson (Section 2) uses Bhaskar's critical realism, which is a different and stronger ontological position. If you cite both, define which you mean, or an examiner will notice.\n• Use the quality-criteria point (p. 114). If you add interviews or document analysis (for example of RBI or bank disclosures), you can justify using trustworthiness criteria for that part and validity and reliability for the econometrics, while saying why mixing is acceptable. This is the 'methods may be used with any paradigm' claim (p. 105).\n• Use Table 6.1 as your methodology chapter's anchor table, cited to p. 109.\nParaphrase it in your own words, since the table is theirs.\n• Honest limit: the authors say positivism and constructivism cannot be logically combined (p. 116). A mixed-methods thesis must therefore argue it sits inside one paradigm (here, postpositivism with qualitative inputs, which the authors themselves describe, p. 110), not across two.\n\nFile flags\n• The PDF is a scan; I read it by OCR. A few words in the OCR output were split or garbled, so check any exact quotation against the page before it goes in the thesis.\n• Table 6.1 above is in my words; the original wording is on p. 109 of your PDF.\n• The chapter is from 1994 and is frequently cited alongside the later Lincoln, Lynham and Guba (2011) update, which is not in your folder.\n\n2. Lawson (1997), Economics and Reality\nWhat it is. Tony Lawson's Economics and Reality (Routledge, 1997) is the founding book of critical realism in economics. Its argument: mainstream economics keeps failing, and the root cause is that economists adopt methods without asking what the social world is like (a neglect of ontology). Lawson proposes a realist alternative drawn from Roy Bhaskar's philosophy."},{"pdf":197,"printed":182,"ch":8,"text":"What your file contains (important). Your PDF is only 39 pages: the front matter and contents, the Preface (pp. xii to xx), all of Chapter 1 (pp. 3 to 14), and the first four pages of Chapter 2 (pp. 15 to 18, ending mid-argument). It is not the whole book. Page numbers below are the book's own printed pages (Roman numerals for the preface). I read every page in the file. The book's later chapters (transcendental realism, econometrics, abstraction, demiregularities, policy) are known to me only from the table of contents, so these notes do not summarise them. If your methodology chapter needs them, you will need the rest of the book.\n\nThe Preface: what the book is trying to do (pp. xii to xx)\n• Starting complaint (p. xii): a newspaper heading, ”'No reality, please. We're economists!'”, captures a widespread view that modern economics is ”largely irrelevant to an understanding of real world matters”. Lawson's response is partial agreement: ”there is a sense in which social reality is neglected” (p. xii).\n• Diagnosis (p. xii to xiii): the remarkable feature is ”the minimal concern that is shown either for tailoring methods to insights available regarding the nature of the social world, or for explicitly determining the sorts of conditions under which chosen methods would be appropriate”. Methods are chosen for ”technical sophistication” or ”conformity with a priori conceptions of proper practice” (p. xii).\n• The key sentence for your methodology chapter (p. xiii): ”contemporary economics is marked by an effective neglect of ontology, by a lack of attention to elaborating the nature of (social) being or existence.” The book ”constitutes a realist theory of and for economics” (p. xiii).\n• Why ontology and not Popper (pp. xiii to xv): economic methodology has been\ndominated by epistemology (how to appraise theories), mainly Popper and Lakatos. Lawson endorses Mäki's view that ”the Popperian dominance has led to a misallocation of intellectual resources in economic methodology” (p. xv).\n• Positivism's two assumptions (p. xv): that science develops in a single (monistic) way, and that it is deductive in structure. Popper undermined the first but left the second untouched. ”It is the presumption of a deductivist structure of science ... that I am concerned here to criticise before anything else” (p. xv).\n• Intellectual debts (pp. xiv to xvi): economists such as Keynes, Marx, Veblen, Hayek, Menger and Shackle already expressed parts of the view (pp. xii to xiv). Of philosophers, Bhaskar ”has managed most successfully” to combine the attack on deductivism with the attack on monism (p. xvi). Bachelard, Lakatos, Kuhn and Feyerabend are named as critics of the monist view (p. xvi). Bachelard is also the source of the ”epistemological break” idea in Bolton et al. (Layer 6), so this is a cross-link.\n• Bottom line (p. xviii): ”the possibility of realising an efficacious and emancipatory science of economics is far from being out of the question”, but it needs ”a greater sensitivity to context and a rather more reflective and critical orientation”.\n• Book map (pp. xvii to xviii): Part I (science and economics), Part II (econometrics, 'theory', subjectivism), Part III (human agency and social structure), Part IV (explanation without experiments), Part V (policy and prediction)."},{"pdf":198,"printed":183,"ch":8,"text":"Chapter 1. Enduring tensions as points of departure (pp. 3 to 14)\nOpening claim (p. 3): ”Contemporary academic economics is not in a healthy state.” Evidence offered: press criticism, and complaints by economists themselves (Hendry's ”Econometrics: Alchemy or Science?”, Leamer's ”Let's take the con out of econometrics”, Kirman's ”The emperor has no clothes”) (pp. 3 to 4). Leontief (1982), quoted on p. 4: journals are filled with ”mathematical formulas leading the reader from sets of more or less plausible but entirely arbitrary assumptions to precisely stated but irrelevant theoretical conclusions”, and econometricians ”fit algebraic functions of all possible shapes to essentially the same sets of data without being able to advance ... a systematic understanding of the structure and the operations of a real economic system.” Lawson's own thesis for the chapter (p. 5): the most revealing problem is not just poor forecasts but theory/practice inconsistencies: economists claim to do one thing and actually do another. He gives three:\n1. At the level of method (pp. 5 to 8). Economists use techniques that contradict the inference theory they cite. McCloskey (p. 5): ”Economists do not follow the laws of enquiry their methodologies lay down.” In econometrics, practitioners ”run” hundreds or thousands of regressions, which contradicts classical inference, and they make ad hoc revisions to forecasts (p. 6). Leamer's image (pp. 6 to 7): a ”celibate priesthood of statistical theorists” and ”a legion of inveterate sinner-data analysts”, often the same people, who ”wantonly sinned in the basement” and then ”metamorphosed into the highest of high priests” upstairs (p. 7). Lawson's reading (p. 7): the real failing is the willingness to live with the gap and to accept ”compromise formulations, ad hoc stratagems”. He questions whether formal inference ”is really required at all” (p. 8).\n2. At the level of social theory (pp. 8 to 11). Economists say people choose, yet the formal models deny choice: ”if real choice means anything it is that any individual could always have acted otherwise. And this is precisely what contemporary 'theorists' are unable to allow” (p. 9). Duesenberry's ”economics is all about how people make choices” (p. 9) is set against models where there is one rational course and it is always followed. Illustrations: Sargent and Wallace's ”conundrum” about policy parameters that become endogenous (pp. 9 to 10), Reder on the ”thrust for endogenisation” (p. 10), and Veblen's ”lightning calculator of pleasures and pains” (pp. 10 to 11).\n3. At the level of methodology (pp. 11 to 14). Economists say methodology is\nuseless but cannot stop doing it. Hahn's reply to Backhouse: ”Methodology like original sin won't go away” and, on learning philosophy, ”whatever next?” (p. 12). The ESRC postgraduate guidelines include a philosophy-of-social-science section for 18 of 20 subjects but not economics (pp. 11 to 12; the count is Lawson's, given as 'one of only two out of the twenty'). Caldwell reports that most US economists are indifferent or hostile to methodology (p. 13). Summary (p. 14): economics ”fares poorly on its own terms; it neither provides particularly accurate forecasts of events nor illuminates the world in which we live”, and the response of more data and software leaves ”the existing ways of doing economics by and large ... untouched”. Therefore ”a rather fundamental, necessarily broadly methodological, enquiry” is needed."},{"pdf":199,"printed":184,"ch":8,"text":"Chapter 2 (first pages only). Realism, explanation and science (pp. 15 to 18)\n• Realism (p. 15): a scientific realism asserts that ”the ultimate objects of scientific investigation exist for the most part quite independent of, or at least prior to, their investigation.” Lawson ties realism to ontology, ”enquiry into the nature of being” (p. 15).\n• Why bother (p. 16): because mainstream economists ”seem merely to assume that whatever the methods employed, social reality must, as it were, 'fit'” (p. 16). A realist position ”insists that methods of social science can, and indeed should, be designed to take account of available insights concerning the nature of social material” (p. 16).\n• Deductivism (pp. 16 to 18): the culprit is the event-regularity view of laws, 'whenever event x then event y', plus the principle that laws are tested by their instances (pp. 16 to 17). Explanation in this view means deducing the explanandum from initial conditions plus universal laws (the covering-law or deductive-nomological model). Explanation and prediction then become the same thing (the ”symmetry thesis”, p. 17). The radiator-ice example (pp. 17 to 18) shows the structure: initial conditions, law, phenomenon.\n• Claim about economics (p. 18): deductivism ”characterises contemporary mainstream economics”; econometrics searches for probabilistic event regularities, and economic theory posits strict conjunctions as axioms. Hahn (1985, quoted p. 18) defends it: ”all theory in all subjects proceeds in this manner.” Lawson's task is to question ”the presumption of the generalised relevance of deductivism” (p. 18).\n• Where your file stops: at the end of p. 18 Lawson is about to ask what the social world must be like for event regularities to hold. His answer (that regularities require a closed system, which the open social world seldom provides) comes later in the book and is not in your file.\n\nHow to use it in the thesis\n• Justify your methodology chapter's ontology paragraph. The sentence on p. xiii (neglect of ontology) lets you state, before any model, what you assume about the banking system and climate risk: that they are open, evolving, and not governed by stable event regularities. Cite pp. xiii and 18.\n• Defend caution about econometric claims. Lawson's p. 6 point (specification search across many regressions) is a standard hazard for panel regressions of capital adequacy on climate exposure. Report the number of specifications you tried, and say why.\n• Link to Layer 6. Bolton et al. argue that climate risk is not captured by history-based models; Monasterolo lists non-linearity, deep uncertainty and endogeneity as features of climate risk (p. 307); Lawson supplies the philosophical grounding for why such models fail (deductivism, p. 18). Bachelard appears in both Lawson (p. xvi) and Bolton.\n• Link to Guba and Lincoln (Section 1). Guba and Lincoln classify paradigms by ontology, epistemology and methodology; Lawson argues ontology comes first. Together they let you name your position (critical realism) explicitly.\n• Careful framing. Lawson is a heterodox critic. For an economics examiner, present critical realism as the reason for mixed-method triangulation and for modest causal claims, not as a rejection of econometrics."},{"pdf":200,"printed":185,"ch":8,"text":"File flags\n• The PDF is an excerpt (preface, Chapter 1 and four pages of Chapter 2). Do not attribute to Lawson any later argument on the strength of this file.\n• The scan quality is good, but the text extraction occasionally splits words (for example 'w i t h'). Check quotes against the PDF.\n\n3. Master quotables and thesis methodology links\nQuotables (check exact wording against the page before it goes in the thesis)\nTable 8.4. Master quotables and thesis methodology links: Quotables (check exact wording against the page before it goes in the thesis)\n\nSource and page\n\nQuote or close paraphrase\n\nGuba and Lincoln, Questions of method are secondary to\np. 105\nquestions of paradigm, the basic belief\nsystem that guides the investigator in\nontologically and epistemologically\nfundamental ways\n\nOpening your methodology\nchapter\n\nGuba and Lincoln, Both qualitative and quantitative\np. 105\nmethods may be used appropriately with\nany research paradigm\n\nJustifying mixed inputs\n\nGuba and Lincoln, A paradigm is a set of basic beliefs that\np. 107\ndeals with ultimates or first principles,\naccepted on faith however well argued\n\nDefining paradigm\n\nGuba and Lincoln, The methodological question cannot be\np. 108\nreduced to a question of methods;\nmethods must be fitted to a\npredetermined methodology\n\nMethod vs methodology\ndistinction\n\nGuba and Lincoln, Postpositivism: reality exists but is only\np. 110\nimperfectly apprehendable; objectivity\nremains a regulatory ideal\n\nStating your own paradigm\n\nGuba and Lincoln, The ontological position most\np. 111\ndifferentiates constructivism from the\nother three paradigms\n\nExplaining why you did not\nchoose constructivism\n\nGuba and Lincoln, Rigor criteria depend on the realist\np. 114\nontological position; without it,\nisomorphism with reality, strict\ngeneralizability and objectivity lose\nmeaning\n\nWhy validity language is\nparadigm-bound\n\nGuba and Lincoln, Realism and relativism, value freedom and Limits of mixing paradigms p. 116 value boundedness, cannot coexist in any internally consistent metaphysical system\n\nGuba and Lincoln, No inquirer ought to go about the\np. 116\nbusiness of inquiry without being clear\nabout just what paradigm informs and\nguides his or her approach\n\n185\n\nUse it for\n\nClosing line for a paradigm\nparagraph"},{"pdf":201,"printed":186,"ch":8,"text":"Source and page\n\nQuote or close paraphrase\n\nUse it for\n\nLawson, p. xii\n\nChapter-heading style complaint: ”No\nreality, please. We're economists!”\n\nFraming the ontology gap\n\nLawson, p. xiii\n\nEconomics has neglected ontology and\nOntology-first justification\nneeds a realist theory of and for economics\n\nLawson, p. 4\n(quoting Leontief\n1982: 104)\n\nPage after page of journals filled with\nCritique of formalism (use as\nmathematical formulas leading from\na secondary quote: cite\nplausible but arbitrary assumptions to\nLeontief via Lawson)\nprecisely stated but irrelevant conclusions;\neconometricians fit functions to the same\ndata without advancing systematic\nunderstanding of a real economic system\n\nLawson, pp. 6 to 7 Economists divide into a celibate\n(quoting Leamer) priesthood of statistical theorists and a\nlegion of inveterate sinner-data analysts\n\nLawson, p. 18\n(quoting Hahn\n1985: 9)\n\nCritics of theory as tautologous are read\nas not liking the axioms; all theory in all\nsubjects proceeds by deduction from\naxioms and assumptions\n\nGap between econometric\ntheory and practice\nThe deductivism assumption\nmainstream economists\nrarely question\n\nRule for 12 to 14: these are quotations of other authors. In the thesis cite them as ”Leontief (1982: 104), quoted in Lawson (1997: 4)” unless you check the originals.\n\nHow Layer 7 feeds the thesis methodology chapter\n1. Paradigm paragraph. Open with Guba and Lincoln's three questions (p. 108), then answer them for your study. A defensible answer: ontology, a real but imperfectly knowable financial system (postpositivist, p. 110); epistemology, modified objectivist, findings probabilistic; methodology, falsification-oriented empirical testing with a documented qualitative classification step.\n2. Ontology-first justification. Lawson (p. xiii) gives you the argument that method choice should follow a view of what social reality is. Use it to explain why climate transition risk, which is deeply uncertain and non-ergodic (Layer 6, Monasterolo), cannot be treated as a stable law-like regularity.\n3. Critical realism, defined once. Pick Lawson's (Bhaskar's) sense or the Guba and Lincoln / Cook and Campbell sense, and say which. Lawson's gives you deep structures and open systems; the other is a mild form of postpositivism. They are not the same.\n4. Deductivism critique and your econometrics. Lawson (pp. 16 to 18) says deductivism needs closed systems and constant event regularities. Your capital-adequacy regressions and stress tests assume some stability. State it as a limitation and as the reason you treat results as conditional, not as laws.\n5. Triangulation and mixed methods. Guba and Lincoln describe critical multiplism as refurbished triangulation (p. 110). Use that to justify combining panel regression, scenario or stress-test outputs and policy-document evidence, within one postpositivist paradigm (their own limit on cross-paradigm mixing is p. 116).\n6. Quality criteria. Name the criteria you apply: internal and external validity, reliability and objectivity for the quantitative part; credibility and dependability for any coding of"},{"pdf":202,"printed":187,"ch":8,"text":"disclosures (pp. 114 to 115).\n\nLinks back to earlier layers\n• Layer 6 (Bolton et al.): the ”epistemological break” with Bachelard (Lawson also cites Bachelard, p. xvi) supports your claim that backward-looking risk models cannot capture climate risk.\n• Layer 6 (Monasterolo): nonlinearity, deep uncertainty and endogeneity are ontological claims about the economy; Lawson's open-system argument is the philosophical backing.\n• Layers 3 and 5 (econometrics and stress testing): Lawson's Chapter 1 critique (Leamer, Leontief) is the prepared answer to an examiner asking why you trust regression output.\n• Layers 2 and 4: use Guba and Lincoln's Table 6.1 to place the empirical studies you reviewed (most are positivist or postpositivist) and to explain why you follow them.\n\nWhat was read and what was not\nTable 8.5. Master quotables and thesis methodology links: What was read and what was not Source\n\nRead\n\nNot read or not in your file\n\nGuba and\nLincoln\n(1994)\n\nWhole chapter pp. 105 to 116\n(OCR of a scan)\n\nReference list p. 117\n\nLawson\n(1997)\n\nPreface pp. xii to xx; Chapter 1 pp. The rest of Chapter 2 and Chapters 3 onward, 3 to 14; Chapter 2 pp. 15 to 18 including his full account of transcendental realism and open and closed systems. Do not cite Lawson for claims beyond p. 18 on the basis of these notes"},{"pdf":203,"printed":188,"ch":8,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 7"},{"pdf":204,"printed":189,"ch":8,"text":""},{"pdf":205,"printed":190,"ch":8,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 7"},{"pdf":206,"printed":191,"ch":8,"text":""}],"cards":[]};

window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.ln=window.CHTML.ln||{};window.CHTML.ln[8]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Before you start</h1>\n<p data-b=\"2\"><strong>Why this layer exists.</strong> Layers 1 to 6 gave you the content of the thesis. Layer 7 gives you the philosophical footing for the methodology chapter: what kind of knowledge claims you are entitled to make, and why. It is short, but examiners ask about it.</p>\n<p data-b=\"3\"><strong>Six terms to know first</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"4\"><strong>Ontology:</strong> what exists; what the nature of (economic) reality is.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"5\"><strong>Epistemology:</strong> how we can know it; the relationship between researcher and subject.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"6\"><strong>Methodology vs methods:</strong> methodology is the logic of how you find out; methods are the tools (regression, interviews, stress tests).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"7\"><strong>Positivism / postpositivism:</strong> reality is out there and can be measured (positivism) or only imperfectly and probabilistically (postpositivism).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"8\"><strong>Critical realism:</strong> two meanings here. Lawson (via Bhaskar): a layered, open-system reality. Guba and Lincoln: a label for postpositivism.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"9\"><strong>Constructivism:</strong> realities are multiple and socially constructed; inquiry is interpretive.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"10\"><strong>Map of the two readings</strong></p>\n<table data-b=\"11\">\n<caption><strong>Table 8.1.</strong> Before you start</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Reading</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Role</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Size and condition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln (1994)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Compares four paradigms; gives you the vocabulary and the Table 6.1 to anchor your chapter</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">13-page scan, read by OCR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lawson (1997)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Argues economics neglects ontology; critiques deductivism and econometric practice</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">39-page excerpt only (preface, Ch. 1, start of Ch. 2)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"12\"><strong>Suggested reading order.</strong> Read Section 1 (Guba and Lincoln) first for the vocabulary, then Section 2 (Lawson) for the economics-specific argument, then Section 3 for quotables and how to write it into the thesis. About 40 minutes.</p>\n<p data-b=\"13\"><strong>One warning.</strong> The two authors use \"critical realism\" differently, and Guba and Lincoln openly argue for constructivism. Neither is a neutral summary.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"14\" id=\"b14\">1. Guba and Lincoln (1994), Competing Paradigms in Qualitative Research</h1>\n<p data-b=\"15\"><strong>What it is.</strong> Chapter 6 of Denzin and Lincoln’ s <em>Handbook of Qualitative Research</em> (Sage, 1994), pp. 105 to 117. It compares four research paradigms (positivism, postpositivism, critical theory, constructivism) by asking each three questions: what is reality, how can we know it, and how do we find out. It is the most-cited paradigm chart in the social sciences.</p>\n<p data-b=\"16\"><strong>Read in full:</strong> the whole chapter, pp. 105 to 116 (p. 117 is the reference list). Your PDF is a photocopy scan without a text layer, so I read it by OCR and checked the key page (p. 109, with Table 6.1) against the page image. Page numbers are the Handbook’ s own printed pages.</p>\n<p data-b=\"17\"><strong>Bias to keep in mind:</strong> the authors state at once that they are committed to constructivism (p. 105), so the chapter argues from that side. Read the descriptions of positivism and postpositivism as how a constructivist sees them.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"18\" id=\"b18\">The argument in six moves</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"19\"><strong>Paradigm comes before method (p. 105).</strong> The authors argue \"questions of method are secondary to questions of paradigm, which we define as the basic belief system or worldview that guides the investigator, not only in choices of method but in ontologically and epistemologically fundamental ways.\" Both qualitative and quantitative methods \"may be used appropriately with any research paradigm\" (p. 105). So a regression is not automatically positivist, and an interview is not automatically constructivist.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"20\"><strong>Why quantification dominated (pp. 105 to 106).</strong> Mathematics is the \"queen of sciences\"; fields that quantify are called \"hard\"; the social sciences copied them, partly after Mill’ s urging and partly for status (p. 106).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"21\"><strong>Two kinds of critique of the \"received view\" (pp. 106 to 107).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"22\"><em>Intraparadigm (internal) critiques</em> (p. 106): context stripping; exclusion of meaning and purpose; the gap between grand etic theory and local emic views; inapplicability of general data to individual cases (nomothetic vs idiographic); and exclusion of the discovery dimension. These can be met by adding qualitative data.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"23\"><em>Extraparadigm (external) critiques</em> (p. 107), which the authors say weigh more: the <strong>theory-ladenness of facts</strong> (facts are facts only within a theoretical framework), the <strong>underdetermination of theory</strong> (the problem of induction: \"a million white swans can never establish ... that all swans are white, one black swan can completely falsify it\"), the <strong>value-ladenness of facts</strong>, and the <strong>interactive nature of the inquirer and the inquired-into</strong> (Heisenberg and Bohr cited as precedent).</p></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"24\"><strong>What a paradigm is (pp. 107 to 108).</strong> \"A paradigm may be viewed as a set of basic beliefs (or metaphysics) that deals with ultimates or first principles\" (p. 107); the beliefs \"must be accepted simply on faith (however well argued)\" (p. 107). Each paradigm answers three linked questions, and each answer constrains the next (p. 108):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"25\"><strong>Ontological:</strong> what is the form and nature of reality?</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"26\"><strong>Epistemological:</strong> what is the relationship between knower and known?</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"27\"><strong>Methodological:</strong> how can the inquirer find out? \"The methodological question cannot be reduced to a question of methods; methods must be fitted to a predetermined methodology\" (p. 108).</p></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"28\"><strong>Paradigms are human constructions (p. 108).</strong> There is no ultimate proof of one over another; advocates \"must rely on persuasiveness and utility rather than proof\" (p. 108). The authors add that this does not doom them to radical relativism.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"29\"><strong>The four paradigms compared (Table 6.1, p. 109; elaborated pp. 109 to 112).</strong> Shown below in my wording of their table.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<table data-b=\"30\">\n<caption><strong>Table 8.2.</strong> Guba and Lincoln (1994): The argument in six moves</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Positivism</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Postpositivism</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Critical theory et al.</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Constructivism</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ontology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Naive realism: a real reality that can be apprehended</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Critical realism: a real reality, but only imperfectly and probabilistically apprehended</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Historical realism: a \"virtual\" reality shaped by social, political, cultural, economic, ethnic and gender values, crystallised over time</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Relativism: local and specific constructed realities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Epistemology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Dualist and objectivist; findings true</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Modified dualist/objectivist; critical tradition and community; findings probably true</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Transactional and subjectivist; findings value-mediated</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Transactional and subjectivist; findings created</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Methodology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Experimental and manipulative; verification of hypotheses; chiefly quantitative</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Modified experimental; critical multiplism; falsification of hypotheses; may include qualitative methods</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Dialogic and dialectical</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hermeneutical and dialectical</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"31\"><strong>Positivism</strong> (pp. 109 to 110): reality \"driven by immutable natural laws and mechanisms\", knowledge as \"time- and context-free generalizations\", research \"can, in principle, converge on the ’ true’ state of affairs\"; the stance is \"reductionist and deterministic\" (p. 109). Inquiry is \"as through a one-way mirror\" (p. 110).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"32\"><strong>Postpositivism</strong> (p. 110): reality exists but is \"only imperfectly apprehendable because of basically flawed human intellectual mechanisms\"; the label <em>critical realism</em> comes from Cook and Campbell (1979). Objectivity \"remains a ’ regulatory ideal’\"; replicated findings are \"probably true\"; methodology uses \"critical multiplism (a refurbished version of triangulation)\" to falsify rather than verify.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"33\"><strong>Critical theory</strong> (pp. 110 to 111): a blanket term including neo-Marxism, feminism, materialism and participatory inquiry. Structures were once plastic and then \"crystallized (reified)\" into what is now taken as natural. The investigator’ s values inevitably shape findings, which \"effectively challenges the traditional distinction between ontology and epistemology\" (p. 110).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"34\"><strong>Constructivism</strong> (pp. 110 to 111): realities are \"multiple, intangible mental constructions\" held by individuals and groups; constructions are \"not more or less ’ true,’ in any absolute sense, but simply more or less informed and/or sophisticated\". The aim is a \"consensus construction\" more informed than its predecessors (p. 111).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"35\"><strong>Where the real differences lie (p. 111):</strong> \"It is the ontological position that most differentiates constructivism from the other three paradigms\", and \"It is their epistemological positions that most differentiate critical theory and constructivism from the other two paradigms.\"</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"36\" id=\"b36\">Ten practical issues (Table 6.2, p. 112; discussion pp. 112 to 116)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"37\">The authors show that paradigm choice changes how you work. Selected rows:</p>\n<table data-b=\"38\">\n<caption><strong>Table 8.3.</strong> Guba and Lincoln (1994): Ten practical issues (Table 6.2, p. 112; discussion pp. 112 to 116)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Issue</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Positivism and postpositivism</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Critical theory</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Constructivism</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Aim of inquiry (p. 113)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Explanation, leading to prediction and control</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Critique and transformation; restitution and emancipation</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Understanding and reconstruction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nature of knowledge (p. 113)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Verified hypotheses as facts or laws (postpositivism: non-falsified, probable)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Structural and historical insights</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Consensus constructions; multiple knowledges can coexist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Accumulation (pp. 113 to 114)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Accretion, \"building blocks\" in an \"edifice of knowledge\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Historical revision</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">More informed constructions; transfer by vicarious experience (case studies)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Quality criteria (p. 114)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Internal validity, external validity, reliability, objectivity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Historical situatedness; erosion of ignorance; stimulus to action</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Trustworthiness (credibility, transferability, dependability, confirmability) and authenticity criteria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Values (p. 114)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Excluded (\"value free\")</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Included, formative</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Included, formative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Voice (p. 115)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Disinterested scientist\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Transformative intellectual\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Passionate participant\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"39\"><strong>A point worth quoting (p. 114):</strong> the conventional rigor criteria \"depend on the realist ontological position; without the assumption, isomorphism of findings with reality can have no meaning, strict generalizability ... is impossible, stability cannot be assessed ... and objectivity cannot be achieved\". This is the authors’ argument that validity language is tied to a paradigm.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"40\"><strong>Constructivist criteria (p. 114):</strong> the trustworthiness criteria parallel the positivist ones, which the authors admit makes them \"suspect\", and they say quality criteria for constructivism are \"not well resolved\".</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"41\"><strong>Ethics (pp. 114 to 115):</strong> for the first two paradigms ethics is \"extrinsic\" (policed by committees), and the realist ontology provides \"a tilt toward the use of deception\" in some cases; for the other two it is \"intrinsic\".</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"42\"><strong>Training (p. 115):</strong> novices must be \"resocialized\" from the received view, which requires thorough schooling in positivism and postpositivism first.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"43\"><strong>Commensurability (pp. 115 to 116):</strong> positivists and postpositivists think paradigms can be accommodated. Critical theorists and constructivists say they are incommensurable: \"Realism and relativism, value freedom and value boundedness, cannot coexist in any internally consistent metaphysical system\" (p. 116). Resolution awaits \"a metaparadigm that renders the older, accommodated paradigms not less true, but simply irrelevant\" (p. 116).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"44\"><strong>Hegemony (p. 116):</strong> postpositivists \"control publication outlets, funding sources, promotion and tenure mechanisms, dissertation committees\" (p. 116). Worth noting when you discuss how economics treats qualitative work.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"45\" id=\"b45\">Conclusion (p. 116)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"46\">The \"paradigm wars\" metaphor is \"overdrawn\", and the authors favour dialogue. Closing sentence: \"Paradigm issues are crucial; no inquirer, we maintain, ought to go about the business of inquiry without being clear about just what paradigm informs and guides his or her approach.\" Note 1 (p. 116) says postpositivism is \"damage control\" rather than a reformulation, and credits Kuhn (1962) for making the need for a paradigm shift visible.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"47\" id=\"b47\">How to use it in the thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"48\"><strong>State your paradigm in one paragraph.</strong> Your thesis models capital adequacy against climate exposure with panel data and event-style tests, and builds a green/brown classification. In Guba and Lincoln’ s terms, that is postpositivist: a real world, imperfectly known, tested by falsification, with objectivity as a regulatory ideal (pp. 109 to 110). Say so, and say what that means for what you can claim (probable, not proven).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"49\"><strong>Watch the two meanings of ’ critical realism’.</strong> Guba and Lincoln use the label for postpositivism (Cook and Campbell, p. 110). Lawson (Section 2) uses Bhaskar’ s critical realism, which is a different and stronger ontological position. If you cite both, define which you mean, or an examiner will notice.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"50\"><strong>Use the quality-criteria point (p. 114).</strong> If you add interviews or document analysis (for example of RBI or bank disclosures), you can justify using trustworthiness criteria for that part and validity and reliability for the econometrics, while saying why mixing is acceptable. This is the ’ methods may be used with any paradigm’ claim (p. 105).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"51\"><strong>Use Table 6.1 as your methodology chapter’ s anchor table</strong>, cited to p. 109. Paraphrase it in your own words, since the table is theirs.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"52\"><strong>Honest limit:</strong> the authors say positivism and constructivism cannot be logically combined (p. 116). A mixed-methods thesis must therefore argue it sits inside one paradigm (here, postpositivism with qualitative inputs, which the authors themselves describe, p. 110), not across two.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"53\" id=\"b53\">File flags</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"54\">The PDF is a scan; I read it by OCR. A few words in the OCR output were split or garbled, so check any exact quotation against the page before it goes in the thesis.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"55\">Table 6.1 above is in my words; the original wording is on p. 109 of your PDF.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"56\">The chapter is from 1994 and is frequently cited alongside the later Lincoln, Lynham and Guba (2011) update, which is not in your folder.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 data-b=\"57\" id=\"b57\">2. Lawson (1997), Economics and Reality</h1>\n<p data-b=\"58\"><strong>What it is.</strong> Tony Lawson’ s <em>Economics and Reality</em> (Routledge, 1997) is the founding book of critical realism in economics. Its argument: mainstream economics keeps failing, and the root cause is that economists adopt methods without asking what the social world is like (a neglect of <em>ontology</em>). Lawson proposes a realist alternative drawn from Roy Bhaskar’ s philosophy.</p>\n<p data-b=\"59\"><strong>What your file contains (important).</strong> Your PDF is only 39 pages: the front matter and contents, the Preface (pp. xii to xx), all of Chapter 1 (pp. 3 to 14), and the first four pages of Chapter 2 (pp. 15 to 18, ending mid-argument). It is <strong>not</strong> the whole book. Page numbers below are the book’ s own printed pages (Roman numerals for the preface). I read every page in the file. The book’ s later chapters (transcendental realism, econometrics, abstraction, demi-regularities, policy) are known to me only from the table of contents, so these notes do not summarise them. If your methodology chapter needs them, you will need the rest of the book.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"60\" id=\"b60\">The Preface: what the book is trying to do (pp. xii to xx)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"61\"><strong>Starting complaint (p. xii):</strong> a newspaper heading, \"’ No reality, please. We’ re economists!’\", captures a widespread view that modern economics is \"largely irrelevant to an understanding of real world matters\". Lawson’ s response is partial agreement: \"there is a sense in which social reality is neglected\" (p. xii).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"62\"><strong>Diagnosis (p. xii to xiii):</strong> the remarkable feature is \"the minimal concern that is shown either for tailoring methods to insights available regarding the nature of the social world, or for explicitly determining the sorts of conditions under which chosen methods would be appropriate\". Methods are chosen for \"technical sophistication\" or \"conformity with a priori conceptions of proper practice\" (p. xii).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"63\"><strong>The key sentence for your methodology chapter (p. xiii):</strong> \"contemporary economics is marked by an effective neglect of ontology, by a lack of attention to elaborating the nature of (social) being or existence.\" The book \"constitutes a realist theory of and for economics\" (p. xiii).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"64\"><strong>Why ontology and not Popper (pp. xiii to xv):</strong> economic methodology has been dominated by epistemology (how to appraise theories), mainly Popper and Lakatos. Lawson endorses Mäki’ s view that \"the Popperian dominance has led to a misallocation of intellectual resources in economic methodology\" (p. xv).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"65\"><strong>Positivism’ s two assumptions (p. xv):</strong> that science develops in a single (monistic) way, and that it is deductive in structure. Popper undermined the first but left the second untouched. \"It is the presumption of a deductivist structure of science ... that I am concerned here to criticise before anything else\" (p. xv).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"66\"><strong>Intellectual debts (pp. xiv to xvi):</strong> economists such as Keynes, Marx, Veblen, Hayek, Menger and Shackle already expressed parts of the view (pp. xii to xiv). Of philosophers, Bhaskar \"has managed most successfully\" to combine the attack on deductivism with the attack on monism (p. xvi). Bachelard, Lakatos, Kuhn and Feyerabend are named as critics of the monist view (p. xvi). Bachelard is also the source of the \"epistemological break\" idea in Bolton et al. (Layer 6), so this is a cross-link.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"67\"><strong>Bottom line (p. xviii):</strong> \"the possibility of realising an efficacious and emancipatory science of economics is far from being out of the question\", but it needs \"a greater sensitivity to context and a rather more reflective and critical orientation\".</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"68\"><strong>Book map (pp. xvii to xviii):</strong> Part I (science and economics), Part II (econometrics, ’ theory’, subjectivism), Part III (human agency and social structure), Part IV (explanation without experiments), Part V (policy and prediction).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"69\" id=\"b69\">Chapter 1. Enduring tensions as points of departure (pp. 3 to 14)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"70\"><strong>Opening claim (p. 3):</strong> \"Contemporary academic economics is not in a healthy state.\" Evidence offered: press criticism, and complaints by economists themselves (Hendry’ s \"Econometrics: Alchemy or Science?\", Leamer’ s \"Let’ s take the con out of econometrics\", Kirman’ s \"The emperor has no clothes\") (pp. 3 to 4).</p>\n<p data-b=\"71\"><strong>Leontief (1982), quoted on p. 4:</strong> journals are filled with \"mathematical formulas leading the reader from sets of more or less plausible but entirely arbitrary assumptions to precisely stated but irrelevant theoretical conclusions\", and econometricians \"fit algebraic functions of all possible shapes to essentially the same sets of data without being able to advance ... a systematic understanding of the structure and the operations of a real economic system.\"</p>\n<p data-b=\"72\"><strong>Lawson’ s own thesis for the chapter (p. 5):</strong> the most revealing problem is not just poor forecasts but <strong>theory/practice inconsistencies</strong>: economists claim to do one thing and actually do another. He gives three:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"73\"><strong>At the level of method (pp. 5 to 8).</strong> Economists use techniques that contradict the inference theory they cite. McCloskey (p. 5): \"Economists do not follow the laws of enquiry their methodologies lay down.\" In econometrics, practitioners \"run\" hundreds or thousands of regressions, which contradicts classical inference, and they make ad hoc revisions to forecasts (p. 6). Leamer’ s image (pp. 6 to 7): a \"celibate priesthood of statistical theorists\" and \"a legion of inveterate sinner-data analysts\", often the same people, who \"wantonly sinned in the basement\" and then \"metamorphosed into the highest of high priests\" upstairs (p. 7). Lawson’ s reading (p. 7): the real failing is the willingness to live with the gap and to accept \"compromise formulations, ad hoc stratagems\". He questions whether formal inference \"is really required at all\" (p. 8).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"74\"><strong>At the level of social theory (pp. 8 to 11).</strong> Economists say people choose, yet the formal models deny choice: \"if real choice means anything it is that any individual could always have acted otherwise. And this is precisely what contemporary ’ theorists’ are unable to allow\" (p. 9). Duesenberry’ s \"economics is all about how people make choices\" (p. 9) is set against models where there is one rational course and it is always followed. Illustrations: Sargent and Wallace’ s \"conundrum\" about policy parameters that become endogenous (pp. 9 to 10), Reder on the \"thrust for endogenisation\" (p. 10), and Veblen’ s \"lightning calculator of pleasures and pains\" (pp. 10 to 11).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"75\"><strong>At the level of methodology (pp. 11 to 14).</strong> Economists say methodology is useless but cannot stop doing it. Hahn’ s reply to Backhouse: \"Methodology like original sin won’ t go away\" and, on learning philosophy, \"whatever next?\" (p. 12). The ESRC postgraduate guidelines include a philosophy-of-social-science section for 18 of 20 subjects but not economics (pp. 11 to 12; the count is Lawson’ s, given as ’ one of only two out of the twenty’). Caldwell reports that most US economists are indifferent or hostile to methodology (p. 13).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p data-b=\"76\"><strong>Summary (p. 14):</strong> economics \"fares poorly on its own terms; it neither provides particularly accurate forecasts of events nor illuminates the world in which we live\", and the response of more data and software leaves \"the existing ways of doing economics by and large ... untouched\". Therefore \"a rather fundamental, necessarily broadly methodological, enquiry\" is needed.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"77\" id=\"b77\">Chapter 2 (first pages only). Realism, explanation and science (pp. 15 to 18)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"78\"><strong>Realism (p. 15):</strong> a scientific realism asserts that \"the ultimate objects of scientific investigation exist for the most part quite independent of, or at least prior to, their investigation.\" Lawson ties realism to <strong>ontology</strong>, \"enquiry into the nature of being\" (p. 15).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"79\"><strong>Why bother (p. 16):</strong> because mainstream economists \"seem merely to assume that whatever the methods employed, social reality must, as it were, ’ fit’\" (p. 16). A realist position \"insists that methods of social science can, and indeed should, be designed to take account of available insights concerning the nature of social material\" (p. 16).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"80\"><strong>Deductivism (pp. 16 to 18):</strong> the culprit is the <em>event-regularity</em> view of laws, ’ whenever event x then event y’, plus the principle that laws are tested by their instances (pp. 16 to 17). Explanation in this view means <em>deducing</em> the explanandum from initial conditions plus universal laws (the covering-law or deductive-nomological model). Explanation and prediction then become the same thing (the \"symmetry thesis\", p. 17). The radiator-ice example (pp. 17 to 18) shows the structure: initial conditions, law, phenomenon.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"81\"><strong>Claim about economics (p. 18):</strong> deductivism \"characterises contemporary mainstream economics\"; econometrics searches for probabilistic event regularities, and economic theory posits strict conjunctions as axioms. Hahn (1985, quoted p. 18) defends it: \"all theory in all subjects proceeds in this manner.\" Lawson’ s task is to question \"the presumption of the generalised relevance of deductivism\" (p. 18).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"82\"><strong>Where your file stops:</strong> at the end of p. 18 Lawson is about to ask what the social world must be like for event regularities to hold. His answer (that regularities require a closed system, which the open social world seldom provides) comes later in the book and is not in your file.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"83\" id=\"b83\">How to use it in the thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"84\"><strong>Justify your methodology chapter’ s ontology paragraph.</strong> The sentence on p. xiii (neglect of ontology) lets you state, before any model, what you assume about the banking system and climate risk: that they are open, evolving, and not governed by stable event regularities. Cite pp. xiii and 18.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"85\"><strong>Defend caution about econometric claims.</strong> Lawson’ s p. 6 point (specification search across many regressions) is a standard hazard for panel regressions of capital adequacy on climate exposure. Report the number of specifications you tried, and say why.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"86\"><strong>Link to Layer 6.</strong> Bolton et al. argue that climate risk is not captured by history-based models; Monasterolo lists non-linearity, deep uncertainty and endogeneity as features of climate risk (p. 307); Lawson supplies the philosophical grounding for why such models fail (deductivism, p. 18). Bachelard appears in both Lawson (p. xvi) and Bolton.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"87\"><strong>Link to Guba and Lincoln (Section 1).</strong> Guba and Lincoln classify paradigms by ontology, epistemology and methodology; Lawson argues ontology comes first. Together they let you name your position (critical realism) explicitly.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"88\"><strong>Careful framing.</strong> Lawson is a heterodox critic. For an economics examiner, present critical realism as the reason for mixed-method triangulation and for modest causal claims, not as a rejection of econometrics.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"89\" id=\"b89\">File flags</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"90\">The PDF is an excerpt (preface, Chapter 1 and four pages of Chapter 2). Do not attribute to Lawson any later argument on the strength of this file.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"91\">The scan quality is good, but the text extraction occasionally splits words (for example ’ w i t h’). Check quotes against the PDF.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 data-b=\"92\" id=\"b92\">3. Master quotables and thesis methodology links</h1>\n<h2 data-b=\"93\" id=\"b93\">Quotables (check exact wording against the page before it goes in the thesis)</h2>\n<table data-b=\"94\">\n<caption><strong>Table 8.4.</strong> Master quotables and thesis methodology links: Quotables (check exact wording against the page before it goes in the thesis)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">#</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source and page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote or close paraphrase</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Use it for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 105</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Questions of method are secondary to questions of paradigm, the basic belief system that guides the investigator in ontologically and epistemologically fundamental ways</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Opening your methodology chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 105</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Both qualitative and quantitative methods may be used appropriately with any research paradigm</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justifying mixed inputs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 107</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A paradigm is a set of basic beliefs that deals with ultimates or first principles, accepted on faith however well argued</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining paradigm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 108</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The methodological question cannot be reduced to a question of methods; methods must be fitted to a predetermined methodology</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Method vs methodology distinction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 110</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Postpositivism: reality exists but is only imperfectly apprehendable; objectivity remains a regulatory ideal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stating your own paradigm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 111</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The ontological position most differentiates constructivism from the other three paradigms</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Explaining why you did not choose constructivism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 114</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Rigor criteria depend on the realist ontological position; without it, isomorphism with reality, strict generalizability and objectivity lose meaning</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Why validity language is paradigm-bound</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 116</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Realism and relativism, value freedom and value boundedness, cannot coexist in any internally consistent metaphysical system</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Limits of mixing paradigms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln, p. 116</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">No inquirer ought to go about the business of inquiry without being clear about just what paradigm informs and guides his or her approach</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Closing line for a paradigm paragraph</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lawson, p. xii</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Chapter-heading style complaint: \"No reality, please. We’ re economists!\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Framing the ontology gap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">11</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lawson, p. xiii</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Economics has neglected ontology and needs a realist theory of and for economics</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Ontology-first justification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lawson, p. 4 (quoting Leontief 1982: 104)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Page after page of journals filled with mathematical formulas leading from plausible but arbitrary assumptions to precisely stated but irrelevant conclusions; econometricians fit functions to the same data without advancing systematic understanding of a real economic system</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Critique of formalism (use as a secondary quote: cite Leontief via Lawson)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">13</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lawson, pp. 6 to 7 (quoting Leamer)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Economists divide into a celibate priesthood of statistical theorists and a legion of inveterate sinner-data analysts</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Gap between econometric theory and practice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">14</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lawson, p. 18 (quoting Hahn 1985: 9)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Critics of theory as tautologous are read as not liking the axioms; all theory in all subjects proceeds by deduction from axioms and assumptions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The deductivism assumption mainstream economists rarely question</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"95\">Rule for 12 to 14: these are quotations of other authors. In the thesis cite them as \"Leontief (1982: 104), quoted in Lawson (1997: 4)\" unless you check the originals.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"96\" id=\"b96\">How Layer 7 feeds the thesis methodology chapter</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"97\"><strong>Paradigm paragraph.</strong> Open with Guba and Lincoln’ s three questions (p. 108), then answer them for your study. A defensible answer: ontology, a real but imperfectly knowable financial system (postpositivist, p. 110); epistemology, modified objectivist, findings probabilistic; methodology, falsification-oriented empirical testing with a documented qualitative classification step.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"98\"><strong>Ontology-first justification.</strong> Lawson (p. xiii) gives you the argument that method choice should follow a view of what social reality is. Use it to explain why climate transition risk, which is deeply uncertain and non-ergodic (Layer 6, Monasterolo), cannot be treated as a stable law-like regularity.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"99\"><strong>Critical realism, defined once.</strong> Pick Lawson’ s (Bhaskar’ s) sense or the Guba and Lincoln / Cook and Campbell sense, and say which. Lawson’ s gives you deep structures and open systems; the other is a mild form of postpositivism. They are not the same.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"100\"><strong>Deductivism critique and your econometrics.</strong> Lawson (pp. 16 to 18) says deductivism needs closed systems and constant event regularities. Your capital-adequacy regressions and stress tests assume some stability. State it as a limitation and as the reason you treat results as conditional, not as laws.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"101\"><strong>Triangulation and mixed methods.</strong> Guba and Lincoln describe critical multiplism as refurbished triangulation (p. 110). Use that to justify combining panel regression, scenario or stress-test outputs and policy-document evidence, within one postpositivist paradigm (their own limit on cross-paradigm mixing is p. 116).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"102\"><strong>Quality criteria.</strong> Name the criteria you apply: internal and external validity, reliability and objectivity for the quantitative part; credibility and dependability for any coding of disclosures (pp. 114 to 115).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"103\" id=\"b103\">Links back to earlier layers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"104\"><strong>Layer 6 (Bolton et al.):</strong> the \"epistemological break\" with Bachelard (Lawson also cites Bachelard, p. xvi) supports your claim that backward-looking risk models cannot capture climate risk.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"105\"><strong>Layer 6 (Monasterolo):</strong> nonlinearity, deep uncertainty and endogeneity are ontological claims about the economy; Lawson’ s open-system argument is the philosophical backing.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"106\"><strong>Layers 3 and 5 (econometrics and stress testing):</strong> Lawson’ s Chapter 1 critique (Leamer, Leontief) is the prepared answer to an examiner asking why you trust regression output.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"107\"><strong>Layers 2 and 4:</strong> use Guba and Lincoln’ s Table 6.1 to place the empirical studies you reviewed (most are positivist or postpositivist) and to explain why you follow them.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"108\" id=\"b108\">What was read and what was not</h2>\n<table data-b=\"109\">\n<caption><strong>Table 8.5.</strong> Master quotables and thesis methodology links: What was read and what was not</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Read</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Not read or not in your file</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Guba and Lincoln (1994)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Whole chapter pp. 105 to 116 (OCR of a scan)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reference list p. 117</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Lawson (1997)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Preface pp. xii to xx; Chapter 1 pp. 3 to 14; Chapter 2 pp. 15 to 18</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The rest of Chapter 2 and Chapters 3 onward, including his full account of transcendental realism and open and closed systems. Do not cite Lawson for claims beyond p. 18 on the basis of these notes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","s":[[177,"b1"],[178,"b14"],[181,"b57"],[185,"b92"]]};